This is strange. Hope other members can figure out, from the error message, where things go wrong

How about back to the basics and examining the data? E.g., examine the correlations between AgeatTest, TesttoDeath, and a few other variables in the same regression equation? Extremely high correlation? Nearly zero variance?

You use the default missing handling method, which is listwise deletion. Do you data have missing data? If yes, maybe the cases included are very different due to missing data in AgeatTest and TesttoDeath? Usually, we examine the sample size in the summary of sem() but it seems that you cannot do this because sem() failed to run.

You can use na.omit() and nrow() to check the listwise sample size for different combinations of the variables:

``` r
dat <- data.frame(x = c(1, 3, 5, NA, 4),
                  y = c(2, NA, 3, 4, NA),
                  z = c(1, 3, 5, NA, 5))
nrow(dat)
#> [1] 5
nrow(na.omit(dat))
#> [1] 2
nrow(na.omit(dat[, c("x", "y")]))
#> [1] 2
nrow(na.omit(dat[, c("x", "z")]))
#> [1] 4
```

<sup>Created on 2023-07-03 with [reprex v2.0.2](https://reprex.tidyverse.org)</sup>

-- Shu Fai

