Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/UeQzDpLd7z0/m/TGrnWPR3BQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi group
first) i need some one to approve the sequence i have used in measurment invaraince for binary data. Of course, I have modified the commands of this link <https://rdrr.io/cran/semTools/man/measEq.syntax.html>. second) as i find for binary data rsultes of weak and strong model have the same degree of freedom and the same results, so we just run one of them, Is this conclusion correct? third) can we ignore intercepts for analysis because they are always fixed on zero?

test.seq <- list(strong = c("thresholds","loadings","intercepts"),
                 strict = "residuals",
                 means = "means",
                 lv.variances="lv.variances")
meq.list <- list()
for (i in 0:length(test.seq)) {
  if (i == 0L) {
    meq.label <- "configural"
    group.equal <- ""
    long.equal <- ""
  } else {
    meq.label <- names(test.seq)[i]
    group.equal <- unlist(test.seq[1:i])
    # long.equal <- unlist(test.seq[1:i])
  }
  meq.list[[meq.label]] <- measEq.syntax(configural.model = bin.mod,
                                         data = CSBD_B_G,
                                         ordered = paste0("CSBD", 1:9),
                                         parameterization = "theta",
                                         ID.fac = "std.lv <http://std.lv/>",
                                         ID.cat = "Wu.Estabrook.2016",
                                         group = "Gender",
                                         group.equal = group.equal,
                                         #longFacNames = longFacNames,
                                         #long.equal = long.equal,
                                         return.fit = TRUE)
}

compareFit(meq.list)

