An illustration of how to test the mean difference. It is based on the example from a previous of this thread, with three groups instead of two:

``` r
library(lavaan)
#> This is lavaan 0.6-16
#> lavaan is FREE software! Please report any bugs.

cov_list <- list(gp1 = lav_matrix_lower2full(c(1, .49, 1, .49, .49, 1)),
                 gp2 = lav_matrix_lower2full(c(1, .49, 1, .49, .49, 1)),
                 gp3 = lav_matrix_lower2full(c(1, .49, 1, .49, .49, 1)))

cov_list <- lapply(cov_list, function(x) {
    rownames(x) <- colnames(x) <- paste0("x", 1:3)
    x
  })
cov_list
#> $gp1
#>      x1   x2   x3

#> x1 1.00 0.49 0.49
#> x2 0.49 1.00 0.49
#> x3 0.49 0.49 1.00

#>
#> $gp2
#>      x1   x2   x3

#> x1 1.00 0.49 0.49
#> x2 0.49 1.00 0.49
#> x3 0.49 0.49 1.00

#>
#> $gp3
#>      x1   x2   x3

#> x1 1.00 0.49 0.49
#> x2 0.49 1.00 0.49
#> x3 0.49 0.49 1.00
mean_vec <- list(gp1 = c(x1 = .1, x2 = .1, x3 = .1),
                 gp2 = c(x1 = .2, x2 = .2, x3 = .2),
                 gp3 = c(x1 = .4, x2 = .4, x3 = .4))
n <- 200

mod1 <-
"
f1 =~ x1 + x2 + x3
f1 ~ c(m1, m2, m3) * 1
# For identification: Set latent mean of gp2 to zero
m1 == 0
# Manually test mean difference between gp2 and gp3

m3m2 := m3 - m2
"

fit1 <- cfa(mod1,

           sample.nobs = c(n, n, n),
           sample.cov = cov_list,
           sample.mean = mean_vec,

           group.equal = c("intercepts", "loadings"))
fit1
#> lavaan 0.6.16 ended normally after 31 iterations
#>
#>   Estimator                                         ML
#>   Optimization method                           NLMINB
#>   Number of model parameters                        30
#>   Number of equality constraints                    11
#>
#>   Number of observations per group:
#>     gp1                                            200
#>     gp2                                            200
#>     gp3                                            200
#>
#> Model Test User Model:
#>
#>   Test statistic                                 0.000
#>   Degrees of freedom                                 8
#>   P-value (Chi-square)                           1.000
#>   Test statistic for each group:
#>     gp1                                          0.000
#>     gp2                                          0.000
#>     gp3                                          0.000
est1 <- parameterEstimates(fit1)

# Factor means
est1[((est1$lhs == "f1") & (est1$op == "~1")) | est1$op == ":=", ]

#>     lhs op   rhs block group label est    se     z pvalue ci.lower ci.upper

#> 4    f1 ~1           1     1    m1 0.0    NA    NA     NA       NA       NA
#> 15   f1 ~1           2     2    m2 0.1 0.081 1.232  0.218   -0.059    0.259
#> 26   f1 ~1           3     3    m3 0.3 0.082 3.647  0.000    0.139    0.461
#> 35 m3m2 := m3-m2     0     0  m3m2 0.2 0.082 2.452  0.014    0.040    0.360

# Compare CIs:
# m1 vs. m2: Overlap?

est1[15, "ci.upper"] > est1[26, "ci.lower"]
#> [1] TRUE

# Set the mean of f1 to zero in gp2
mod2 <-
"
f1 =~ x1 + x2 + x3
f1 ~ c(m1, m2, m3) * 1
# For identification: Set latent mean of gp2 to zero
m2 == 0
# Manually test mean difference between gp1 and gp3

m3m1 := m3 - m1
"

fit2 <- cfa(mod2,

           sample.nobs = c(n, n, n),
           sample.cov = cov_list,
           sample.mean = mean_vec,

           group.equal = c("intercepts", "loadings"))
fit2
#> lavaan 0.6.16 ended normally after 31 iterations
#>
#>   Estimator                                         ML
#>   Optimization method                           NLMINB
#>   Number of model parameters                        30
#>   Number of equality constraints                    11
#>
#>   Number of observations per group:
#>     gp1                                            200
#>     gp2                                            200
#>     gp3                                            200
#>
#> Model Test User Model:
#>
#>   Test statistic                                 0.000
#>   Degrees of freedom                                 8
#>   P-value (Chi-square)                           1.000
#>   Test statistic for each group:
#>     gp1                                          0.000
#>     gp2                                          0.000
#>     gp3                                          0.000
est2 <- parameterEstimates(fit2)

# Factor means
est2[((est2$lhs == "f1") & (est2$op == "~1")) | est2$op == ":=", ]

#>     lhs op   rhs block group label  est    se      z pvalue ci.lower ci.upper

#> 4    f1 ~1           1     1    m1 -0.1 0.081 -1.232  0.218   -0.259    0.059
#> 15   f1 ~1           2     2    m2  0.0 0.000     NA     NA    0.000    0.000
#> 26   f1 ~1           3     3    m3  0.2 0.082  2.452  0.014    0.040    0.360
#> 35 m3m1 := m3-m1     0     0  m3m1  0.3 0.082  3.647  0.000    0.139    0.461
# Compare CIs:
# m1 vs. m3: Overlap?
est2[4, "ci.upper"] > est2[26, "ci.lower"]
#> [1] TRUE

# Compare the results
est1[((est1$lhs == "f1") & (est1$op == "~1")) | est1$op == ":=", ]

#>     lhs op   rhs block group label est    se     z pvalue ci.lower ci.upper

#> 4    f1 ~1           1     1    m1 0.0    NA    NA     NA       NA       NA
#> 15   f1 ~1           2     2    m2 0.1 0.081 1.232  0.218   -0.059    0.259
#> 26   f1 ~1           3     3    m3 0.3 0.082 3.647  0.000    0.139    0.461
#> 35 m3m2 := m3-m2     0     0  m3m2 0.2 0.082 2.452  0.014    0.040    0.360
est2[((est2$lhs == "f1") & (est2$op == "~1")) | est2$op == ":=", ]

#>     lhs op   rhs block group label  est    se      z pvalue ci.lower ci.upper

#> 4    f1 ~1           1     1    m1 -0.1 0.081 -1.232  0.218   -0.259    0.059
#> 15   f1 ~1           2     2    m2  0.0 0.000     NA     NA    0.000    0.000
#> 26   f1 ~1           3     3    m3  0.2 0.082  2.452  0.014    0.040    0.360
#> 35 m3m1 := m3-m1     0     0  m3m1  0.3 0.082  3.647  0.000    0.139    0.461

```

<sup>Created on 2023-07-29 with [reprex v2.0.2](https://reprex.tidyverse.org)</sup>

As already explained in previous post, with the latent mean of one group (1st group by default) fixed to zero, the other means can be interpreted as the difference in means compared to the reference group. However, with three groups, we need to manually define the difference (m3m2 := m3 - m2). As illustrated above, the SE and pvalue (0.082, .014) are exactly identical to those of the "mean" of the 3rd group (m3 in fit2) by setting the 2nd group as the reference group. This is expected, because this mean of the 3rd group is still a mean difference, but compared to the 2nd group in fit2.

This example also illustrates why CI overlap should not be used. In fit1, with 1st group as the reference group, CIs for the 2nd group and the 3rd group are available, and overlap (-0.059-0.259 vs. 0.139 -0.461). However, both the self-computed difference (m3m2 := m3 - m2) and the alternative model (fit2) show that the latent means of the 2nd and 3rd groups are significantly different.

Therefore, with 7 groups, if necessary, we can do pairwise comparison using only one model, and compute the difference as shown above. Certainly, just like an usual ANOVA, there is a risk of Type I error inflation in these (post hoc?) comparison. Some methods need to be used to control the Type I error rate.

My two cents.

-- Shu Fai

